<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Suspected low rectal cancer in a 41-year-old professional golfer with three months of rectal bleeding, narrower stools, urgency and tenesmus, a firm ulcerated mass felt on the posterior wall of the lower rectum at about 5 cm, iron deficiency anemia (hemoglobin 11.0 g/dL, ferritin 9 ng/mL) and CEA 5.8 ng/mL. Colonoscopy with biopsy is booked this week. Please see her within two weeks, with colorectal surgery, to review the results with her and plan staging and treatment if cancer is found. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Urgent, within two weeks. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>What is the diagnosis and its stage, and what further tests are needed?</item><item>What treatment do you recommend, in what order, and can her rectum and anal sphincter be kept?</item><item>What genetic testing is needed, on the tumor and on her blood, and what would the results mean for her and her family?</item><item>What side effects should primary care watch for during treatment, and what monitoring should we share?</item><item>Can she keep training and competing during treatment, and what should she know about sexual health, contraception and fertility?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. Didrikson Zaharias, 41, is seen at the request of Dr. Vasquez-Thornbury two weeks after she presented with three months of rectal bleeding, a change in bowel habit and a rectal mass. Since then she has had a colonoscopy with biopsy, rectal-protocol MRI, CT of the chest, abdomen and pelvis, and review at our colorectal tumor board. She still passes a little bright red blood most days, with urgency, and tires late in a round. She has pain during sex and spotting afterwards. She is fully active (ECOG 0), has a levonorgestrel IUD and is not planning a pregnancy. She was seen by the colorectal surgeon this morning. She asked whether she would need a colostomy and whether she could keep playing. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Mildred Ella Didrikson was born in Port Arthur, Texas, on June 26, 1911, the sixth of seven children of the Norwegian immigrants Ole Nickolene and Hannah Marie (Olson) Didriksen; she later changed the spelling of her surname. In 1915 the family moved to Beaumont, Texas. [D]</item><item>Her birth year is given variously: she wrote 1914 on her application for the 1932 Olympics, and some sources, including a historical marker at her grave, give 1913, 1914 or 1915; her gravestone and baptismal certificate support 1911. [D]</item><item>At the 1932 Olympic Games in Los Angeles she won gold medals in the javelin and the 80-meter hurdles and a silver medal in the high jump, where her head-first technique was ruled a foul. [D]</item><item>She met the professional wrestler George Zaharias at the 1938 Los Angeles Open, and they married on December 23, 1938; he became her manager. [D]</item><item>She took up golf in 1933, won 17 amateur tournaments in a row between 1943 and 1947, co-founded the Ladies Professional Golf Association in 1949, and was voted the Associated Press Woman Athlete of the Half-Century in 1950. About 1950 she and her husband bought the Tampa Golf and Country Club in Florida and moved into its converted clubhouse. [D]</item><item>By the end of the 1952 golf season she was feeling extremely fatigued. [D]</item></list><paragraph>6 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

